00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:16.279
The term “helicopter airline” is one that
most are probably not familiar with.

00:00:16.279 --> 00:00:22.089
Simply, it’s an airline, transporting passengers
or cargo, that uses helicopters.

00:00:22.089 --> 00:00:27.929
Of course, not all helicopter companies are
truly airlines, since many, if not most, civilian

00:00:27.929 --> 00:00:33.239
applications of helicopters do not involve
transport—the people or things inside often

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:36.200
land right back where they took off.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.990
Helicopter airlines have been around almost
as long as helicopters themselves, but they

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:42.629
certainly are not widespread.

00:00:42.629 --> 00:00:47.510
There was, however, a time when helicopters
were believed to be the future of short-haul

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:51.070
passenger transport, so what happened?

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:55.390
The opportunity for helicopter airlines emerged
around the 1950’s.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:01:00.350
When passenger aviation first began, cities’
first airports were located rather close to

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:04.549
the cities themselves, as they just didn’t
need as much space.

00:01:04.549 --> 00:01:09.299
Around the mid-century, New York’s primary
airport was LaGuardia, Chicago’s was Midway,

00:01:09.299 --> 00:01:14.799
DC’s was National, Paris’ was Le Bourget,
and Tokyo’s was Haneda—all airports fairly

00:01:14.799 --> 00:01:16.810
close to their city centers.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:21.590
These airports were relatively small and generally
had short runways, but that wasn't a problem

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:25.469
because passenger demand was small and airplanes
were too.

00:01:25.469 --> 00:01:29.680
After all, the smaller the airplane, the shorter
the runway needed, and vice versa.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.810
These small airports even worked for the long-haul
flights of the time since, in general, before

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:39.759
the 1950s, these were done on relatively small
airplanes that would make multiple stops on

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:41.130
longer journeys.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:45.670
However, with the introduction of the de Havilland
Comet in the 1950s, and other passenger jet

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:50.641
aircraft soon after, airplanes were growing
larger, range was increasing, and passenger

00:01:50.641 --> 00:01:52.700
demand rose too.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:57.259
That meant that all the small airports of
the past wouldn’t cut it and so, cities

00:01:57.259 --> 00:02:01.579
looked to build larger airports more suited
for the jet age.

00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:05.149
New York’s primary airport shifted from
LaGuardia to JFK, Chicago’s to O’Hare,

00:02:05.149 --> 00:02:10.610
DC’s to Dulles, Paris’ to Orly, and Tokyo’s
to Narita.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:14.730
Each of these larger airports were further
from their city centers, though, as that was

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.580
where there was space.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:21.600
In many cases, the smaller, closer airports
remained in operation for domestic or regional

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.630
flights, but in other cases they closed down.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:29.780
That’s how we got to the reality today where
many cities’ airports are an hour’s trip

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.330
from the city center.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:36.290
Of course, the impact of this distancing varied
depending on the type of flights passengers

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:37.400
were taking.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.900
For example, with a seven-hour flight from
New York to London, the additional 45 or so

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:46.450
minutes it would take to get from Manhattan
to JFK, over LaGuardia, only extends overall

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.890
travel time by about 7.5%.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:54.140
However, on the one-hour flight from New York
to DC, this would extend travel time by nearly

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:55.670
20%.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:59.880
This trend of distancing of airports from
their cities had little impact on long-haul

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.700
flights, but it had a huge effect on short-haul
flights meaning that planes were now less

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.110
competitive compared to cars or trains.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:13.090
Nowadays, via JFK, it takes just 30 minutes
more to travel from Manhattan to downtown

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:17.850
Charlotte than from Manhattan to downtown
DC, even though Charlotte is more than twice

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:18.850
as far.

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:24.040
There are always diminishing marginal returns
to air travel’s efficiency as distance decreases,

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:29.050
but moving airports further from cities increased
this to the point that, below a couple hundred

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:34.810
miles of distance, airplanes are not the fastest
means of travel.

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.080
Helicopter airlines were supposed to solve
that.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:42.710
It was quite the niche business model concept,
but the 1950’s, 60’s, and 70’s saw a

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:48.040
small wave of these particularly in Los Angeles,
San Francisco, Chicago, and New York.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:54.030
SFO airlines, for example, operated an extensive
network around the bay area serving San Jose,

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:59.580
Palo Alto, San Francisco Airport, Oakland
Airport, downtown Oakland, downtown San Francisco,

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.940
Marin City, Berkley, and Concord.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:08.470
SFO Airlines’ fares were quite reasonable—to
go from Berkley to SFO cost just $9 which,

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:12.270
inflation adjusted, is the equivalent of $75
today.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:17.129
With a flight time of 10 minutes and prices
comparable to a taxi, the choice for the consumer

00:04:17.129 --> 00:04:18.129
was easy.

00:04:18.129 --> 00:04:22.129
This route network was primarily intracity,
all flights were within the same metro area,

00:04:22.129 --> 00:04:27.990
but the hypothetical next phase of this business
model’s expansion was for intercity flights.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:32.560
The idea was that helicopters could replace
or augment short-haul flights as a quick way

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.120
to get between city centers.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.000
From New York’s Downtown Manhattan Heliport,
which is just a ten minute walk from the New

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.400
York Stock Exchange, one could run regular
passenger flights reaching Philadelphia in

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:47.630
30 minutes, Baltimore in 60 minutes, and DC
in 70.

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:52.550
From Wall Street, the fastest one could reasonably
travel door to door to DC in a car, train,

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:55.740
or plane would be about four hours.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:00.930
With a passenger helicopter leaving steps
away, that would be cut down to two.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:06.150
So, helicopters offered the possibility of
intracity travel for the same price as taxis,

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:12.419
and intercity travel in half the time—with
so much promise and potential, what went wrong?

00:05:12.419 --> 00:05:17.750
Well, it turns out that it wasn’t so much
that anything major went wrong, but rather

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:22.340
that, when they started, a lot of things went
right.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:27.810
Most specifically, the helicopter industry
in the US was supported by significant federal

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:28.810
subsidies.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:35.490
Up until 1965, the federal government pumped
almost half a billion dollars, inflation adjusted,

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:40.499
into passenger helicopter service in Los Angeles,
San Francisco, Chicago, and New York.

00:05:40.499 --> 00:05:46.599
This made it relatively easy for these companies
to break even, but then, in 1965, the subsidies

00:05:46.599 --> 00:05:48.039
stopped coming.

00:05:48.039 --> 00:05:53.319
It was then all and exclusively up to the
helicopter airlines themselves to turn a profit,

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:56.730
and that’s where the problems started.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.330
Helicopters are tremendously expensive.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:04.710
A Bell 206, for example—one of the most
common passenger helicopters—costs about

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:10.470
$600 per flight hour to operate in terms of
fuel, maintenance, and other variable costs.

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:15.810
On top of that, there’s the cost to buy
it, millions of dollars, the cost of the pilot,

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:19.620
hundreds of dollars per hour, the landing
fees, up to hundreds of dollars per flight,

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:23.129
and the cost to run the company managing the
helicopter.

00:06:23.129 --> 00:06:27.310
This all means that a helicopter like this
will cost in the thousands of dollars per

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:32.499
hour to operate, but it can only seat between
four and seven people.

00:06:32.499 --> 00:06:34.680
That math is simple.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:39.439
Helicopter companies rely on a steady stream
of people who value an hour saved in the many

00:06:39.439 --> 00:06:41.520
hundreds of dollars.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.510
That market certainly exists, but at least
in the 70s and 80s, it proved too small to

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:51.919
keep helicopter airlines afloat without subsidies,
especially after a spate of high-profile accidents

00:06:51.919 --> 00:06:56.680
highlighted the increased danger in comparison
to airplanes and drove passengers away.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:03.159
And so, one by one, Los Angeles, San Francisco,
Chicago, and New York’s major helicopter

00:07:03.159 --> 00:07:05.259
airlines each failed.

00:07:05.259 --> 00:07:10.610
What followed was the dark ages of passenger
helicopter airlines.

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:14.830
Market forces whittled these down to just
the strongest in the few markets that could

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:18.860
feasibly support scheduled passenger helicopter
service.

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:22.490
Those certainly do exist, but there are not
many of them.

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:27.699
For example, there has long existed a passenger
helicopter airline operating between the financial

00:07:27.699 --> 00:07:30.550
hub of Hong Kong and the gambling hub of Macau.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:36.819
While the cities are only 40 miles or 65 kilometers
apart, they sit on opposite sides of the Pearl

00:07:36.819 --> 00:07:38.250
River estuary.

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:43.380
The primary means of transportation between
the two is therefore an hour-long ferry, however,

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:47.841
for wealthier individuals, of which there
are plenty in both Hong Kong and Macau, this

00:07:47.841 --> 00:07:52.790
helicopter shuttle connects the two cities
in 15 minutes for about $550.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:57.680
A somewhat similar service exists between
the country of Monaco and its closest airport

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:04.169
in Nice, France—turning a 45 or 60 minute
drive into a 7 minute, $140 flight—and then

00:08:04.169 --> 00:08:08.719
there are a few other less notable examples
of scheduled passenger helicopter airlines,

00:08:08.719 --> 00:08:13.280
including a couple of subsidized services
to isolated places without runways, but for

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:18.370
the most part, few helicopter airlines survived
through the turn of the century.

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:22.659
These dark ages continued until around 2010.

00:08:22.659 --> 00:08:27.449
It was at that time when there started a tiny
but noticeable renaissance of the business

00:08:27.449 --> 00:08:28.449
model.

00:08:28.449 --> 00:08:33.030
One of the early innovators in this resurgence
was Blade, which grew first by offering flights

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:35.479
from Manhattan to New York’s airports.

00:08:35.479 --> 00:08:40.600
Other companies soon followed, including Airbus
and Uber, to set up similar services all around

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:41.640
the world.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:46.190
Airbus’ effort later closed down, but both
Blade and Uber’s services have now been

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:48.240
operating for a number of years.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:52.410
What’s notable about Blade in particular
is that they have partially fulfilled the

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:58.540
vision that helicopter airlines’ innovators
imagined decades ago, but mostly not with

00:08:58.540 --> 00:08:59.540
helicopters.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:03.270
They operate flights that travel outside the
New York metro area directly from Manhattan,

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:10.070
rather than from LaGuardia, Newark, or JFK,
but these are on seaplanes, rather than helicopters.

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:15.190
Leaving from the East River, these reach the
Hamptons in 40 minutes or Nantucket in 70

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:18.840
minutes—both popular getaway spots for wealthy
New Yorkers.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:24.120
Prices are steep, at $800 to the Hamptons
and $1,100 to Nantucket, but this service

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:31.220
acts as some evidence that some markets can
support more expensive service from city centers.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:34.760
The expansion of such services seemed imminent.

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:40.420
Cape Air, after years of trying, was granted
government approval in early 2020 for a one-year

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:45.460
trial period of regularly-scheduled passenger
seaplane flights between downtown Boston and

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:46.940
downtown New York.

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:51.800
With travel times of an hour and fares around
$400, these flights would target a customer

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:56.840
segment much closer to mass-market, but at
the time of writing, the first of these flights

00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:02.380
have yet to be scheduled and the service seemingly
might have become a victim of the pandemic-induced

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:04.380
travel downturn.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:09.650
Seaplanes could be a short-term solution for
interurban air travel bypassing airports,

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:15.050
but longer term, it’s clear that the future
involves tiltrotor vertical takeoff and landing

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:16.050
aircraft.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:20.790
These aircraft take off like a helicopter,
then tilt their propellers 90 degrees to fly

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:22.130
like an airplane.

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:26.530
This allows them to take off without a runway,
but achieve speeds and efficiencies far greater

00:10:26.530 --> 00:10:28.560
than most helicopters.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:34.630
Currently, no such aircraft is in non-military
passenger service, but plenty are in development.

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:39.330
Uber, for example, has plans to launch air
taxi service using such aircraft—initially

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:42.930
in Dallas, Los Angeles, and Melbourne.

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:47.380
It still has huge amounts of regulatory and
technological challenges to surpass before

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:53.070
this is possible, but the future of short-haul
premium air travel seemingly might not involve

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:54.070
airports.

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:58.500
Uber’s plans involve constructing small
“skyports,” as they call them, around

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:02.800
cities, and these could be as simple as a
repurposed parking garage roof.

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:07.940
It’s tough to imagine a near-future where
the masses are flying from city center to

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:12.060
city center as there’s just a natural limit
to scale.

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:16.760
The mass-market solution for faster intercity
travel is clearly high-speed rail, but the

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:21.330
barrier to entry for private companies in
this is enormous considering the infrastructure

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:22.560
requirements.

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:26.910
The beauty of vertical take off and landing
aircraft is the lack of infrastructure needs,

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:30.120
but that does mean operational costs are much
higher.

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:34.620
Costs don’t matter as much, though, because
such services wouldn’t really be competing

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:37.960
against commercial airlines or trains, at
least at first.

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:42.810
Rather, they’d be competing against the
private aviation market since, for business

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:48.500
travelers, they could offer an overall travel
time comparable or better than private jets.

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:53.440
The potential market is quite premium, but
penetrating a small, yet highly lucrative

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:58.830
market is enough to make expensive city center
to city center service worth pursuing for

00:11:58.830 --> 00:12:00.680
certain companies.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:05.560
Whether such a concept will truly be an aspect
of the near-future is yet to be seen, but

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:12.610
if it is, it could help solve the paradoxical
inefficiency of short-haul air travel.

